---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Term from "../../components/Term.astro";

const platforms = [
  { os: "macOS", arch: "Apple Silicon (arm64)", asset: "aimark_darwin_arm64.tar.gz" },
  { os: "macOS", arch: "Intel (amd64)", asset: "aimark_darwin_amd64.tar.gz" },
  { os: "Linux", arch: "x86-64 (amd64)", asset: "aimark_linux_amd64.tar.gz" },
  { os: "Linux", arch: "ARM (arm64)", asset: "aimark_linux_arm64.tar.gz" },
  { os: "Windows", arch: "x86-64 (amd64)", asset: "aimark_windows_amd64.zip" },
  { os: "Windows", arch: "ARM (arm64)", asset: "aimark_windows_arm64.zip" },
];
const releasesUrl = "https://github.com/nsheaps/aiMark/releases/latest";
---

<Layout
  title="Install — aiMark"
  description="Install the aimark CLI in one command, then benchmark your first model in minutes."
>
  <div class="container container--narrow page">
    <h1>Install aiMark</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One small <Term id="inference">CLI</Term>, no account needed. Install it, run
      <code>aimark</code>, and your machine has a <Term id="system-score">System Score</Term> —
      no models to pick, no runtimes to set up.
    </p>

    <h2 id="curl">Option 1 — one-line install (macOS &amp; Linux)</h2>
    <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://aimark.dev/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
      The script detects your OS and CPU, downloads the right binary from GitHub Releases, verifies
      its checksum, and puts <code>aimark</code> on your PATH. You can
      <a href="/install.sh">read the script</a> before running it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="manual">Option 2 — manual download</h2>
    <p>
      Grab a prebuilt binary from <a href={releasesUrl}>GitHub Releases</a>, unpack it, and place
      it on your PATH:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">OS</th>
            <th scope="col">Architecture</th>
            <th scope="col">Asset</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            platforms.map((p) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{p.os}</td>
                <td>{p.arch}</td>
                <td>
                  <a href={releasesUrl} class="mono">
                    {p.asset}
                  </a>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">Checksums (<code>checksums.txt</code>) are published with every release.</p>

    <h2 id="brew">Option 3 — Homebrew</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="badge badge--info">coming soon</span> A Homebrew formula is on the way:
      <code>brew install aimark</code>. Until then, use the curl script or a manual download.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2 id="first-run">Your first benchmark: one word</h2>
    <pre><code>aimark</code></pre>
    <p>
      That's the whole thing. No flags, no model to choose, no runtime to install first. When it
      runs, <code>aimark</code>:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Detects your hardware</strong> — CPU, RAM, GPU and <Term id="vram">VRAM</Term> (or
        <Term id="unified-memory">unified memory</Term> on a Mac). This becomes your
        <Term id="hardware-profile">hardware profile</Term>; anything undetectable degrades to
        "unknown" and never blocks the run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Assigns your <Term id="capability-class">capability class</Term></strong> —
        Compact, Mainstream, Performance, or Ultra, based on usable accelerator memory — and tells
        you why.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Downloads its <Term id="test-asset">test assets</Term></strong> — the pinned
        llama.cpp build for your platform and your class's pinned test model (roughly 1–19 GB
        depending on class), checksum-verified and cached for next time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Runs the fixed program</strong> — chat feel, sustained throughput, and long-context
        cells on the class model, plus an anchor cell and a
        <Term id="validity-check">validity check</Term> — with live
        <Term id="tokens-per-second">tokens-per-second</Term> and <Term id="ttft">TTFT</Term>
        readouts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shows your score card and offers to upload</strong> — your
        <Term id="system-score">System Score</Term> and class rank. No account required:
        <Term id="anonymous-submission">anonymous submissions</Term> get a
        <Term id="claim-token">claim token</Term> so you can manage the result later. The server
        verifies every cell and recomputes the score before it counts.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="muted">
      <code>aimark --yes</code> auto-uploads, <code>aimark --no-upload</code> keeps everything
      local, <code>aimark --json</code> is for scripts.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Advanced mode.</strong> The pick-your-own machinery is still there:
      <code>aimark run sprint-1 --target ollama:llama3.1:8b</code> benchmarks any
      <Term id="model">model</Term> on any <Term id="runtime">runtime</Term> (or a hosted
      <Term id="provider">provider</Term> with your own <Term id="api-key">API key</Term>) against
      the per-suite boards. Advanced runs feed the <a href="/model-fit/">model-fit matrix</a> but
      never the class system leaderboards.
    </div>

    <h2 id="next">Where to next</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <a class="card" href="/leaderboard/systems/mainstream/">
        <h3>See the boards</h3>
        <p>Find your machine's row in its class and see how your hardware stacks up.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/docs/methodology/">
        <h3>Understand your score</h3>
        <p>What ~1000 points means, and how sub-scores roll up into the composite.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/docs/glossary/">
        <h3>Learn the language</h3>
        <p>Every term the CLI just threw at you, defined in plain English.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
